[{:adr/id 2799005411, :adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-5411 — Firefighters Station Equipment-Readiness and Administrative-Logistics Coordination Coverage", :adr/status :accepted, :adr/date "2026-07-17", :adr/doc_type "adr", :adr/source-format "edn", :adr/scope [:kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5411], :adr/references [2607121000 2607152500], :adr/related ["adr-2790003355-cloud-itonami-isco-3355-police-inspectors-detectives-coverage"], :actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-5411, :actor/name "Fire Station Equipment Readiness and Administrative-Logistics Coordination Practice", :actor/domain :public-safety/fire-station-equipment-and-logistics-coordination, :actor/isco-08 "5411", :actor/wave 2, :actor/robotics? true, :actor/coordination? true, :actor/proposal-ops [:log-equipment-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-readiness-concern :coordinate-supply-order], :actor/always-escalate-ops [:flag-readiness-concern], :actor/hard-checks [:crew-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :station-basis :tactical-assessment-forbidden :tactical-assignment-forbidden :scope-exclusion], :actor/scope-excluded-ops [:enter-structure :authorize-structure-entry :commit-to-structure-entry :decide-structure-entry :perform-triage :make-triage-decision :prioritize-casualty-rescue :prioritize-rescue :provide-medical-treatment :administer-medical-treatment :issue-tactical-command :direct-incident-response :issue-incident-command-order], :actor/scope-excluded-terms ["commit to structure entry" "committed to structure entry" "authorize structure entry" "authorized structure entry" "decide to enter the structure" "decided to enter the structure" "enter the structure" "entered the structure" "make the triage prioritization decision" "made the triage prioritization decision" "make the triage decision" "made the triage decision" "make the rescue prioritization decision" "made the rescue prioritization decision" "prioritize the rescue" "prioritized the rescue" "prioritize casualties" "prioritized casualties" "provide medical treatment" "provided medical treatment" "administer medical treatment" "administered medical treatment" "administer treatment" "administered treatment" "issue the tactical command" "issued the tactical command" "issue the incident command order" "issued the incident command order" "direct the incident response" "directed the incident response" "建物進入を決定した" "建物進入を許可した" "建物への進入を決定した" "トリアージの優先順位を決定した" "救助の優先順位を決定した" "医療処置を行った" "応急処置を実施した" "戦術指揮を発令した" "現場指揮命令を発令した"], :actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 500}", :phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-equipment-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:log-equipment-record :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:log-equipment-record :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :flag-readiness-concern}, :auto #{:log-equipment-record :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order}}}", :operation/nodes [:intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold], :operation/entry-point :intake, :registry/maturity :implemented, :registry/required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger], :registry/operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit], :registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5411", :registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-5411", :test/counts "{:total 43, :assertions 150, :failures 0, :errors 0}", :test/coverage [:governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :crew-verification :station-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation :tactical-assessment-forbidden :tactical-assignment-forbidden :rogue-advisor-end-to-end], :test/simulator :firestation.actor-test, :db/id -1, :adr/body "# ADR 2799005411: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 5411 Firefighters station equipment-readiness/administrative-logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 5411\n-- Firefighters** only.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"5411\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"5411\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Firefighters\" -- confirming the\npremise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was\n`:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics :identity\n:audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5411` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`5411` entry to `:implemented`. Under `kotoba.occupation.wave`\n(ADR-2607121000 section 3), sub-major `54` (protective services\nworkers) falls in ISCO Wave 2 (coordination-logistics), consistent with\nthis actor's coordination-only scope.\n\n**This is one of the three highest-stakes occupation actors built in\nthis fleet's session so far, alongside the concurrent siblings\nISCO-5412 (Police Officers) and ISCO-5413 (Prison Guards).**\nFirefighters make life-and-death decisions under severe time pressure:\nwhich structure to enter, how to prioritize casualties/resources when\nthey are scarce, and physical rescue/extraction actions during active,\nrapidly-evolving, physically hazardous incidents. Wrong autonomous\naction in this domain could directly cause loss of life or severe\nphysical harm -- to the firefighter, to a bystander, or to a casualty\nawaiting rescue -- a risk class at least as severe as, and arguably\nmore immediately physical than, the fleet's prior most-cautious\nprecedent, **ADR-2607152500** (Wave4 rollout start amendment), which\nestablished that decisions directly touching a person's safety,\ndignity or subsistence must always exclude the closed op allowlist and\nalways escalate to a human, not merely be gated behind a confidence\nthreshold. The concurrent sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-3355` (Police\nInspectors and Detectives, landed earlier this session) applied an\nanalogous guardrail shape to a due-process/liberty-risk context via a\nclosed four-op allowlist plus content-based scope exclusion; this ADR\nadapts that identical safety-pattern shape to a domain whose failure\nmode is even more immediate and physical: an autonomous system\nmistakenly influencing a structure-entry decision, a triage/rescue-\npriority call, a medical intervention, or a tactical order during an\nactive fire/rescue incident could kill someone within minutes, with no\nopportunity for later correction.\n\nGiven this severity, the guardrail here goes further than \"gate behind\nescalation\": the four categories of decision named in the task\n(structure entry, casualty triage/resource prioritization, medical\ntreatment, and any operational/tactical command during an active\nincident) are **not represented by any op anywhere in this codebase,\ngated or otherwise** -- there is no `:decide-structure-entry` op with\nan `:always-escalate` flag, no `:triage-casualties` op requiring human\nsign-off. The actor's closed proposal-op allowlist contains only four\npurely administrative/logistics ops, and none of them can be\nreconfigured, parameterized, or extended at runtime to touch any of the\nfour excluded categories -- doing so would require modifying and\nredeploying the governor's `closed-op-allowlist` set itself, which\n`GOVERNANCE.md`/`CONTRIBUTING.md` declare a permanent, non-negotiable\nscope boundary.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and for the\nclosed-allowlist + registered-AND-verified provenance + content-based\nscope-exclusion SAFETY PATTERN is the concurrent sibling\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3355`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3355)\n(Police Inspectors and Detectives) -- re-cloned and re-read in full in\nthis session (`store.cljc`/`advisor.cljc`/`governor.cljc`/\n`actor.cljc`/both test files/full doc set), adapted substantially to\nthis vertical's structure-entry/triage/medical-treatment/tactical-\ncommand guardrail rather than the arrest/search/charge/guilt-\ndetermination guardrail it was built for. The `store`/`advisor`/\n`governor`/`actor` module split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph shape,\nand full documentation set (README, business-model, operator-guide,\nGOVERNANCE, CONTRIBUTING, CODE_OF_CONDUCT, SECURITY, AGPL-3.0-or-later\nLICENSE) also traces back to\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals), the fleet's original module-shape\nreference, which `cloud-itonami-isco-3355` itself mirrors.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5411` as an OSS station/\nequipment/administrative-logistics coordination actor for\nfirefighters, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every\nprior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Station Logistics\nAdvisor ⊣ FirestationGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- `5b4e5e70837b849b14b6a5800894e3be202ad5fa` --\na different library coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the\nunrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **station/equipment/administrative-\nlogistics coordination ONLY, never tactical, triage, or medical\nauthority** -- it must NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate\nexercising, or propose exercising ANY of: a decision to enter or not\nenter a hazardous structure, a casualty-triage or resource-\nprioritization decision (deciding who or what gets rescued first when\nresources are limited), a medical-treatment decision, or any\noperational/tactical command decision during an active emergency\nresponse. These are not merely gated behind escalation -- they are\nstructurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely. During an ACTIVE\nemergency incident, this actor's role ends at \"here is the equipment/\npersonnel roster status\" -- it has zero authority over tactical\ndecisions, which remain entirely with the human incident commander, at\nall times, with zero exception.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-equipment-record` -- apparatus/gear readiness and maintenance-\n  status data entry (physical equipment condition/maintenance-status\n  metadata only -- equipment id, timestamp, condition, maintenance\n  status -- NEVER a tactical assessment or an entry decision)\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- shift/training schedule proposal\n  (scheduling logistics only, NEVER an active-incident tactical\n  assignment)\n- `:flag-readiness-concern` -- surface an equipment-deficiency,\n  staffing-shortfall or training-gap concern, ALWAYS escalates\n  immediately, NEVER auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- equipment/apparatus procurement\n  proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles**:\ndeciding to enter or not enter a hazardous structure, making a\ncasualty-triage or resource-prioritization decision, providing medical\ntreatment, or issuing an operational/tactical command decision during\nan active emergency response. These are **structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an op\nsuch as `:enter-structure` cannot even be proposed by the reference\nmock advisor (its `infer` function only ever emits one of the four\nallowlist ops, driven by a `case` expression with no default branch\nproducing any other op shape), and if any code path (a forged\nproposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever produced such\nan op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check (`:op-not-allowed`)\nrejects it unconditionally and permanently. Verified two ways: (a) unit\ntests directly against `governor/check` for thirteen concretely-named\nforbidden op fixtures (`firestation.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:enter-structure`, `:authorize-structure-entry`, `:commit-to-\nstructure-entry`, `:decide-structure-entry`, `:perform-triage`,\n`:make-triage-decision`, `:prioritize-casualty-rescue`, `:prioritize-\nrescue`, `:provide-medical-treatment`, `:administer-medical-\ntreatment`, `:issue-tactical-command`, `:direct-incident-response`,\n`:issue-incident-command-order`), and (b) an END-TO-END test that\ndrives each of those same thirteen ops through a ROGUE advisor and the\nFULL `langgraph.graph` actor graph (not just the governor's pure check\nfunction), proving the StateGraph itself always resolves to `:hold`\nwith zero records committed even when the advisor node itself is\ncompromised\n(`firestation.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`).\n\n### Seven HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Crew unregistered/unverified** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently `:registered?`/`:verified?` crew-member record. Never\n   trusts the proposal's own claim of who the crew member is. This\n   directly implements the task's \"station/crew record must be\n   independently verified/registered before any action\" invariant.\n2. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance -- the\n   governor never dispatches hardware and never itself performs a\n   tactical or medical action).\n3. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op allowlist\n   is a HARD, PERMANENT block. This independently rejects any attempt\n   to slip in an `:enter-structure`/`:make-triage-decision`/`:provide-\n   medical-treatment`/`:issue-tactical-command`-shaped op.\n4. **Station basis** -- `:log-equipment-record`,\n   `:schedule-crew-operation` and `:coordinate-supply-order` must cite\n   a REGISTERED AND VERIFIED station matching the crew member's own\n   station (`:missing-station-id` / `:unknown-station` / `:station-\n   unverified` / `:station-mismatch`). `:flag-readiness-concern` is\n   deliberately EXEMPT from this check -- it is the channel by which a\n   brand-new, not-yet-registered station is surfaced for human intake,\n   so it must not be blocked on the very station record it exists to\n   help create.\n5. **Tactical-assessment forbidden** -- `:log-equipment-record` is a\n   physical equipment condition/maintenance-status metadata record\n   ONLY. Any proposal carrying a tactical/entry-decision field\n   (`:entry-decision`, `:structure-assessment`, `:go-no-go`, `:hazard-\n   clearance`, `:entry-authorized?`, `:structure-entry-decision`) is a\n   HARD, PERMANENT block -- this actor never records a tactical\n   assessment or an entry decision, only physical equipment condition.\n   This check is UNIQUE to this ADR relative to the module-shape\n   reference (`cloud-itonami-isco-3313`) -- the accounting-support\n   domain did not need an equivalent, but the risk here (a\n   \"documentation\" op silently carrying a structure-entry go/no-go\n   call) required it, mirroring the analogous evidentiary-\n   interpretation-forbidden check in the safety-pattern reference\n   (`cloud-itonami-isco-3355`).\n6. **Tactical-assignment forbidden** -- `:schedule-crew-operation` is\n   shift/training scheduling logistics ONLY. Any proposal carrying\n   active-incident tactical-content fields (`:incident-tactical-\n   assignment`, `:triage-assignment`, `:command-order`, `:incident-\n   command-directive`, `:tactical-role-assignment`, `:rescue-\n   priority`) is a HARD, PERMANENT block -- this actor never assigns\n   crew to an active-incident tactical role, only to a shift or\n   training slot. Also UNIQUE to this ADR relative to the module-shape\n   reference, for the same reason as check 5, mirroring the analogous\n   interview-substance-forbidden check in the safety-pattern\n   reference.\n7. **Scope exclusion** -- independent of the op-allowlist check and\n   checks 5/6, any proposal whose free text (`:rationale` or `:note`)\n   names a finalization/execution action for a structure-entry\n   decision, a casualty-triage/rescue-prioritization decision, a\n   medical-treatment decision, or a tactical/operational command (e.g.\n   \"commit to structure entry\", \"made the triage decision\", \"provided\n   medical treatment\", \"issued the tactical command\", plus Japanese\n   equivalents) is a HARD, PERMANENT block, evaluated unconditionally\n   on content. This is defense-in-depth on top of checks 3/5/6: even if\n   a future change somehow widened the allowlist or an allowed op's\n   field shape, content-based rejection still independently blocks\n   tactical/triage/medical/command language wherever it appears in\n   free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-readiness-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless\n  of confidence (governor's `always-escalate-ops`). This is the ONLY\n  channel by which the robot may surface an observation suggesting a\n  station needs human fire-officer attention -- it never resolves the\n  observation itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is the\n  equipment/personnel roster status,\" never \"here is what to do about\n  the fire\" or \"here is who to rescue first.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-escalation-threshold` (500, inclusive boundary verified\n  by `ok-at-exact-supply-cost-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs\n  human sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not\n  a hard block: an over-budget equipment/apparatus order is not itself\n  unsafe, unlike a structure-entry/triage/medical-treatment/tactical-\n  command attempt, which is why it is handled by a different mechanism\n  (soft escalate vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, explicitly called out in this task's guidance)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"entry\", \"triage\", \"treatment\", \"command\") can accidentally match\ninside the mock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate\nproposal, self-blocking the actor's own happy path -- the task's\nguidance named this exact risk class for this occupation and required\nscope-exclusion terms be phrased as finalization/execution ACTIONS\n(\"commit to structure entry\", \"make the triage prioritization\ndecision\", \"provide medical treatment\"), never bare nouns (\"entry\",\n\"triage\"). `firestation.governor/scope-excluded-terms` follows this\nrequirement exactly -- every entry is a multi-word finalization/\nexecution action phrase, never a bare noun -- and\n`firestation.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-\ntrip-scope-exclusion` is a dedicated regression test asserting every\nop in the closed allowlist (including both the station-id-present and\nstation-id-nil variants of `:flag-readiness-concern`), driven through\nthe default mock advisor, never trips `:scope-excluded` or `:op-not-\nallowed`. The mock advisor's own default rationale template\n(\"documented <op> for station <id>\" / \"documented <op> (no station yet\n-- new-station intake)\") was written to avoid any bare-noun collision\nby construction. Green on first run in this session -- no self-trip\nwas actually hit during development, but this ADR still documents the\nguard and its regression test per the task's explicit requirement,\nsince the risk class is especially acute for this domain's vocabulary\n(a legitimate equipment-readiness log necessarily uses words like\n\"entry\" in mundane senses -- e.g. an apparatus-bay entry log -- that a\nbare-noun list would have false-positived on).\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"5411\" ...}` entry promoted\n  from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id` set to\n  the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-5411` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-5411`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately before\n  the edit to account for concurrent sibling landings -- ISCO-5412\n  Police Officers and ISCO-5413 Prison Guards -- in this same batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 43 tests, 150 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- consistent with the live convention already confirmed\n  by sibling ISCO actors (`cloud-itonami-isco-3313`/`-3355` do NOT\n  depend on `kotoba-lang/technology`; only the central `kotoba-lang/\n  occupation` registry itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-\n  technologies` stacks).\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to tx-data\")\n  -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string blob rather\n  than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`/`SECURITY.md` say \"cloud-itonami organization\"\n  for private-contact routing (matching the factually-correct org name\n  under which this repository is published, consistent with the fix\n  independently made by prior sibling ADRs in this batch).\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-structure-entry`/`:approve-\n  triage-decision` that is merely gated behind `always-escalate-ops`\n  (the shape `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for `:approve-over-ceiling-\n  posting`, or the shape a naive reading of \"gate high-stakes actions\n  behind human approval\" might suggest): rejected outright per the\n  explicit task guidance, the `ADR-2607152500` precedent, and the\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3355` precedent -- an always-escalate op still\n  means the *capability* to influence a structure-entry, triage,\n  medical-treatment or tactical-command decision exists in the code\n  path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For a decision this\n  severe and this time-critical (an active fire/rescue incident where\n  seconds matter), the capability itself must not exist, because even\n  an \"always-escalate\" op creates a UI/API surface that a human\n  incident commander could be pressured to rubber-stamp under time\n  pressure, or that a future maintainer could quietly flip to auto-\n  commit. The only op related to readiness escalation is `:flag-\n  readiness-concern`, which surfaces the need for human attention and\n  never itself decides, authorizes, or defers a tactical/medical action\n  for later automatic execution.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"entry\" \"triage\"\n  \"treatment\" \"command\"]`): rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  warning about this fleet-known self-trip bug class -- this actor's\n  own domain necessarily discusses equipment/station/operation concepts\n  (e.g. a \"bay entry log\", a \"training command roster\") to do its\n  documentation job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the\n  actor's own legitimate proposals. The actual implementation avoids\n  this by using full finalization/execution action phrases exclusively.\n- Merging `crew` and `station` into a single entity (only checking one\n  provenance record): rejected -- the cross-station check (`:station-\n  mismatch`) requires two independently registered-AND-verified\n  entities so that a station different from the proposing crew\n  member's own assignment is caught even if both records individually\n  pass verification, matching the two-entity provenance shape already\n  established by the `cloud-itonami-isco-3355` officer+case pattern.\n- Allowing `:log-equipment-record` to carry a brief post-incident\n  equipment-usage OUTCOME field (e.g. a `:deployed?`/`:used-in-\n  incident?` status, as distinct from a tactical assessment):\n  considered, but rejected for this initial implementation -- even a\n  status-only field is a plausible seed for scope creep into recording\n  incident-tactical content over time (a \"deployed\" flag today, a\n  \"deployed during interior attack\" field tomorrow). Equipment-\n  condition logging only for v1; a follow-up ADR could reintroduce a\n  narrowly-scoped, explicitly tactical-content-free status field if a\n  legitimate administrative need is demonstrated, with its own\n  dedicated forbidden-keys guardrail.\n- A numeric confidence-only gate for `:log-equipment-record` /\n  `:schedule-crew-operation` (no forbidden-keys content check):\n  rejected as insufficient -- confidence measures the advisor's\n  certainty about the *proposal it chose to make*, not whether that\n  proposal smuggles forbidden content; a high-confidence advisor could\n  still misuse an allowed op's field shape to carry a forbidden\n  tactical assessment or incident assignment, which is exactly why\n  checks 5/6 exist as independent, content-based, permanent HARD blocks\n  regardless of confidence.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nMirroring the itemized-safety-confirmation format used by the\n`cloud-itonami-isco-3355` (Police Inspectors and Detectives) sibling\nADR earlier this session: every op in this actor's closed proposal-op\nallowlist, and why it is safe under the constraints above.\n\n1. **`:log-equipment-record`** -- physical equipment-condition/\n   maintenance-status metadata only (equipment id, timestamp,\n   condition, maintenance status). Cannot carry a tactical assessment\n   or an entry decision (HARD-blocked by check 5,\n   `:tactical-assessment-forbidden`, on a closed forbidden-key list:\n   `:entry-decision`, `:structure-assessment`, `:go-no-go`, `:hazard-\n   clearance`, `:entry-authorized?`, `:structure-entry-decision`). Not\n   a structure-entry decision, a triage decision, a medical-treatment\n   decision, or a tactical command in any respect -- it is a records-\n   management action indistinguishable in kind from a mechanic's\n   maintenance log.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- shift/training scheduling logistics\n   only (operation type, proposed time, location). Cannot carry an\n   active-incident tactical assignment (HARD-blocked by check 6,\n   `:tactical-assignment-forbidden`, on a closed forbidden-key list:\n   `:incident-tactical-assignment`, `:triage-assignment`, `:command-\n   order`, `:incident-command-directive`, `:tactical-role-assignment`,\n   `:rescue-priority`). Never assigns a crew member to a role in an\n   active emergency response -- it is a calendar action, scoped\n   exclusively to routine shifts and training sessions, never an\n   active incident.\n3. **`:flag-readiness-concern`** -- surfaces an equipment-deficiency,\n   staffing-shortfall, or training-gap concern to a human fire\n   officer; ALWAYS escalates (governor's `always-escalate-ops`,\n   unconditionally, at every confidence level), NEVER auto-commit\n   eligible. Carries only administrative trigger metadata (`:reason`\n   from a closed keyword set, `:note` free text subject to the same\n   scope-exclusion check as every other op's free text). This is the\n   ONLY channel through which a readiness observation may be surfaced,\n   and it explicitly never resolves, decides, or directs anything\n   itself -- the human fire officer reviews and acts entirely on their\n   own authority. It is never used, and can never be used, to surface\n   or recommend a structure-entry, triage, medical, or tactical\n   decision -- it is scoped to equipment/staffing/training readiness\n   only, and the scope-exclusion check (rule 7) additionally rejects\n   any such content smuggled into its `:note` field.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- equipment/apparatus procurement\n   proposal (item, cost, vendor). Not a tactical, triage, or medical\n   action of any kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics action, gated by\n   ordinary cost-ceiling escalation (above 500 always escalates) like\n   any station procurement workflow.\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na decision to enter or not enter a hazardous structure, a casualty-\ntriage or resource-prioritization decision, a medical-treatment\ndecision, or an operational/tactical command decision during an active\nemergency response -- these four categories are structurally absent,\nnot gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself\n(`firestation.governor/closed-op-allowlist`), (b) thirteen concretely-\nnamed forbidden-op fixtures that are rejected at both the governor-unit\nlevel and the full-actor-graph level via a rogue advisor\n(`firestation.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`firestation.governor-test/every-scope-excluded-op-name-is-rejected`,\n`firestation.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`),\n(c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield, phrased exclusively as finalization/execution actions to avoid\nthe fleet-known self-trip bug class\n(`firestation.governor/out-of-scope?`, `firestation.governor/scope-\nexcluded-terms`, with a dedicated regression test\n`firestation.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`),\nand (d) two additional content-shape guardrails unique to this domain\npreventing an otherwise-allowed op from smuggling forbidden substance\nthrough its own field shape (checks 5/6 above). During an active\nemergency incident, this actor's contribution is limited, by\nconstruction, to reporting equipment/personnel roster status to the\nhuman incident commander -- it has no code path, gated or ungated, by\nwhich it could influence a structure-entry, triage, medical, or\ntactical decision.\n"}]
